<text>static int ipvlan_open(struct net_device *dev) {
    ...
    ipvlan_ht_addr_add(port, &ipvlan->addrs, &ipvlan->hwaddr, false);
    ...
}
</text>
Reason: The address hash table is stored per-port (`struct ipvl_port`), but the lock protecting it was per-device (`struct ipvl_dev.addrs_lock`). Multiple ipvlan devices sharing the same port could concurrently call `ipvlan_ht_addr_add()` on the same hash table using different per-device locks, causing race conditions (e.g., false negatives in `ipvlan_addr_busy()`, hash corruption). Additionally, `ipvlan_open()` and `ipvlan_close()` called `ipvlan_ht_addr_add/del` without acquiring any lock (they relied on `rcu_read_lock`, which is insufficient for hash table modifications), allowing races with concurrent IPv6 address updates that occur without `rtnl_lock`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>struct ipvl_port { ... spinlock_t addrs_lock; };
</text>
<text>static int ipvlan_add_addr(struct ipvl_dev *ipvlan, ...) {
    spin_lock_irqsave(&port->addrs_lock, flags);
    ipvlan_ht_addr_add(port, ...);
    ...
}
</text>
<text>static int ipvlan_open(struct net_device *dev) {
    ...
    spin_lock_bh(&port->addrs_lock);
    ipvlan_ht_addr_add(port, &ipvlan->addrs, &ipvlan->hwaddr, false);
    spin_unlock_bh(&port->addrs_lock);
    ...
}
</text>
Reason: The lock is moved from the per-device `ipvl_dev` to the per-port `ipvl_port`, ensuring that all devices sharing the same port use a single lock to protect the shared hash table. This eliminates the race where different devices held different locks while modifying the same hash table. The missing locks in `ipvlan_open`/`ipvlan_close` are added by acquiring `port->addrs_lock` before calling `ipvlan_ht_addr_add/del`, preventing concurrent modifications from IPv6 notifiers that may run without `rtnl_lock`.

